A windowed pi reads its whole log as it opens a conversation and holds every
entry whole for as long as it runs, though the model reads only what the last
compaction keeps: a 38 MB log held 50.8 MB of heap. Read the log light
instead. Entries the kept view leaves out keep only what tells their parts
apart (types, roles, tool call ids and names, usage) and values of 1024
characters or fewer; each longer value is read again from its line when
asked for, as a new object each read, since an appended line never changes.
On that log the heap after opening falls from 50.8 MB to 19.9 MB and the peak
from 159 MB to 109 MB; on a 78 MB log from 103.7 MB to 42.9 MB and from 229 MB
to about 140 MB, with every entry, context and branch read as a whole read
reads it.

Every way pi writes or copies the log reads what it was read light from whole
first: a rewrite, since every line moves, and a move to an entry, which holds
the model's new context whole. A later compaction lets go of what it leaves
out, entries appended since included, but only where pi knows the entry's
place: an append records one only when the file grew by exactly that line,
and noting a place never fails a write. A log pi would migrate, an empty one,
or one whose last line is unfinished is read whole, as unpatched pi reads it.

Apply only to the pi a windowed session runs; a host opens light through its
own runtime.

